{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"20b8d1d8-5397-4bf1-8838-6d05c82b6c1d","hash":"6870ef6ff44a3aaee7613cde1a6f8bcd414f6442f2f143a558bcd6c5bef4e543","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The preset scores 9 of 12 on \"Fix a bug\" against the defaults' 10 of 12, so the row shows red, which is the trade-off this was meant to expose. I'm closing the UI task and committing milestone 1 with ADR-056 next.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KogR34ZVknUrmsUepVuck7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /c/Users/Yunus-Emre/Desktop/portable_agent_kit/projects/modelduck && npm test 2>&1 | grep -q \"^ℹ fail 0\" || { echo \"TESTS FAILED\"; exit 1; }; (cd ../.. && python aikit.py ui finish modelduck --reviewed --reviewer \"Claude (AI; headless Edge review with sample per-type data in a scratch profile: By question type lists each task's types with preset and defaults, tuned and unseen, and marks a type the preset answers worse; no console errors; delegated by the user on 2026-11-01)\" 2>&1 | tail -2) > /tmp/finish-types.out; cat /tmp/finish-types.out; grep -q \"UI task closed\" /tmp/finish-types.out || exit 1; cat >> .aikit/decisions.md <<'EOF'\n\n### ADR-056: Each task's result is broken down by question type\n- **Date**: 2026-11-01\n- **Status**: Accepted\n- **Context**: A task's preset was reported as one rate, so a setting that helped one kind of question (say, writing functions) and hurt another (fixing bugs) looked like a small net change. The benchmark is what sets ModelDuck apart, and its question types were invisible in the tuner.\n- **Decision**: The tune takes `typeOf(itemId)` (the tune job passes each suite item's category), and a task's statistics and its held-back check carry `byType`: per question type, the items and right answers for the chosen settings and for the defaults. Details and a run's detail show them under \"By question type\" in plain words (for example \"Work in a codebase\", \"GSM8K word problems\"), with the types the preset answers worse than the defaults marked.\n- **Consequences**: Per-type counts are small (often under 15), so they show where to look rather than prove a difference; the task-level paired test stays the decision rule.\nEOF\ncd ../.. && python aikit.py check modelduck 2>&1 | grep -q \"4/4 gates passed\" || { echo \"GATE FAILED\"; python aikit.py check modelduck 2>&1 | grep \"   - \"; exit 1; }; python scripts/disruption_recovery.py --workspace projects/modelduck --checkpoint bench-types --agent claude-opus-5.5 --phase verification --phase-num 4 --desc \"Milestone 1 per-type results in; tests pass, gate 4/4, Edge review; checklists: passed\" | tail -1 && MSG='feat(ui): [milestone 1/3] show results by question type\n\nDetails and a run'\"'\"'s detail list right answers per question type for\nthe preset and the defaults, tuned and unseen, and mark the types the\npreset answers worse (ADR-056).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>' && python scripts/disruption_recovery.py --validate-commit \"$MSG\" >/dev/null || exit 1; cd projects/modelduck && git add public/app.js public/styles.css .aikit/ui_history.json .aikit/decisions.md .aikit/task_ledger.json && git commit -q -m \"$MSG\" && git log --oneline -1 && git status --short","description":"Close the UI task, add ADR-056, gate and commit milestone 1","timeout":600000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
